WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.050
On one morning the British cavalry astonished everyone twice on the same plain: first a thin red line halted the Russian horse, then the Heavy Brigade charged uphill and won.

2
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:17.380
That afternoon, four lines on a piece of paper drove six hundred and seventy riders at a gallop into the mouths of the guns.

3
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:25.830
The man who wrote the order, the man who carried it, and the man who obeyed it each insisted to the end of his life that the guilty party was someone else.

4
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:42.960
On the northern shore of the Black Sea, at the southern tip of the Crimean peninsula, there is a narrow inlet that looks as if it had been cut into the rock with a knife.

5
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:53.810
Its mouth is so tight that anyone passing by from the sea would barely notice it; but once you are inside, the water suddenly deepens, steep slopes rise on either hand, and the wind drops away.

6
00:00:53.810 --> 00:01:03.220
In the autumn of 1854 the name of this inlet began to be spoken in the same breath as the fate of the British army: Balaclava.

7
00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.070
The war had not in fact begun here.

8
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:18.200
The conflict that broke out in 1853 between Russia and the Ottoman Empire was still being fought along the Danube and in the Caucasus when the Russian navy destroyed the Ottoman squadron at Sinop and upset the balance of Europe.

9
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.970
In the spring of 1854 Britain and France declared war on Russia.

10
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:29.340
After long argument, the allied staffs settled on a single objective: Sevastopol.

11
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:35.710
If the great naval base that sheltered Russia's Black Sea fleet could be taken, that fleet would never come south again.

12
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:45.120
In September the allied armies landed on the western coast of the Crimea, broke the Russian positions on the river Alma, and swung round to the south of Sevastopol.

13
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:55.810
But instead of taking the city by assault they decided to besiege it, and that decision turned a campaign everyone expected to last a few weeks into something close to a year of trench warfare.

14
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.140
A besieging army also needs a harbour.

15
00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:04.070
The French took the inlets to the west; Balaclava fell to the British.

16
00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.600
Balaclava was small.

17
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:17.130
This inlet, where only a handful of ships could tie up at any one time, had to carry the bread, the powder, the tents, the medicine, and the nosebags of more than 30 thousand men.

18
00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.900
The road from the harbour up to the siege lines climbed in curves between the hills.

19
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:27.470
The entire British army was fed through a single artery, and that artery ran through here.

20
00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:29.680
The Russians knew it.

21
00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:36.530
Understanding the ground that stretches inland to the north of the harbour is the key to understanding everything I am about to describe.

22
00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.460
Picture a flat plain with a low, long ridge running across the middle of it from east to west.

23
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:48.470
A cart track called the Woronzoff Road runs along the top of that ridge; the British named the ridge itself the Causeway Heights.

24
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.800
The Causeway Heights cut the plain in two.

25
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.570
The hollow ground to the south is the South Valley, and it looks down towards the harbour.

26
00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.900
The long corridor to the north is the North Valley.

27
00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:05.790
On the far side of the North Valley, this time from the north, rise the Fedyukhin Heights.

28
00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:13.440
So the North Valley is a corridor squeezed between two walls: the Causeway Heights on the right, the Fedyukhin Heights on the left.

29
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:16.130
Keep that corridor in mind.

30
00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:21.700
Six earth redoubts had been dug along the Causeway Heights to protect the road to the harbour.

31
00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:24.870
Their defence was given to Ottoman soldiers.

32
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:36.680
The easternmost, Number 1, the one nearest the enemy, stood on a rise the British called Canrobert's Hill after the French commander, and it held a garrison of about 600 men, among them Tunisian troops.

33
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.170
The other three redoubts held 300 apiece.

34
00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:47.980
As for guns, there were nine 12-pounders landed from the British fleet: three in Number 1, two in each of the others.

35
00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:53.390
A single British artillery sergeant who knew how to work the guns had been left with each detachment.

36
00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.120
That was all.

37
00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:00.210
The most critical line on the plain was held by light earthworks and a few old guns.

38
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:04.980
The morning of 25 October 1854 was misty and cold.

39
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:15.350
The Russian commander Pavel Liprandi, with a force of roughly 25 thousand men and 78 guns, had advanced in darkness and was drawn up in front of the redoubts as day broke.

40
00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:18.840
Towards 6 o'clock the guns in Number 1 opened fire.

41
00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.170
The Battle of Balaclava had begun.

42
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.660
The Russian artillery bore down on the redoubt.

43
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.110
Under heavy round shot the earth parapets turned into clouds of dust.

44
00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:39.680
The men inside held on for nearly an hour and a half, which was longer than anyone had expected of them, since they had neither adequate guns nor a line to fall back on.

45
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:44.610
Towards half past seven the Russian infantry came into the works with the bayonet.

46
00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:52.740
About 170 of the garrison were killed; the rest went over the back wall and ran down into the South Valley, towards the harbour.

47
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.390
Cossack horsemen followed after them.

48
00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:00.200
Once Number 1 had fallen, the others could not be held.

49
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.170
Numbers 2, 3 and 4 were abandoned in turn.

50
00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:13.580
As the men withdrew they tried to hammer spikes into the touch holes of the guns, but the Russians still carried off seven pieces as trophies and took them to Sevastopol.

51
00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:22.990
This was a far more important detail than it looks; in that age, for an army to lose its guns to the enemy was very nearly the same disgrace as losing its colours.

52
00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:30.320
For a reader with a newspaper in London, the phrase losing guns carried exactly the weight it carried for an officer at the front.

53
00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.490
At half past eight the situation was this.

54
00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.820
The Causeway Heights were in Russian hands.

55
00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.990
The Woronzoff Road was cut.

56
00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:43.320
There was no defensive line in the middle of the plain.

57
00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:51.450
And between the harbour of Balaclava and the Russian cavalry there stood nothing but a few hundred metres of open ground and a single regiment.

58
00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:56.860
That regiment was the 93rd Highlanders, under the command of Sir Colin Campbell.

59
00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:04.510
Campbell was 62, not an aristocrat, a soldier who had earned his promotions slowly and on battlefields.

60
00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:11.040
His regiment was posted on a low hummock a little beyond the small settlement of Kadikoi, in front of the harbour.

61
00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:21.890
He had under his hand some 550 Highlanders, about a hundred lightly wounded men who had joined them, a few dozen guardsmen, and a portion of the Ottoman soldiers who had come back from the redoubts.

62
00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:31.300
About 400 horsemen detached themselves from the Russian cavalry, crossed the Causeway Heights, came down into the South Valley and made straight for the harbour.

63
00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:38.630
What infantry were supposed to do in the face of a cavalry charge was written plainly in the drill books of the day: form square.

64
00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:45.000
Closed in on itself in four ranks, infantry became a kind of thorn wall that horses would not ride into.

65
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.530
Campbell did not form square.

66
00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:51.820
He left his regiment in two ranks, as a long thin line.

67
00:06:51.820 --> 00:07:01.870
The reason was simple: in square his frontage would shrink, with a shorter frontage his firepower would drop, and with less firepower the road to the harbour behind him would be open.

68
00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.760
He took the risk.

69
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:14.450
The words he spoke to his men as he rode along the line became one of the most often repeated sentences in British military memory: there was no retreat from here, they would die where they stood.

70
00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:18.420
The Russian horsemen came on at a trot.

71
00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:22.230
The first volley was fired at long range and did little.

72
00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:27.640
The second came at closer range; horses stumbled, the line broke up.

73
00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:32.890
At that very moment a part of the Highlanders made as if to spring forward and chase the enemy.

74
00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:38.780
What Campbell shouted at them also went into the record: 93rd, damn all that eagerness.

75
00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:45.150
The men stayed where they were, fired a third volley, and the Russian cavalry turned aside and withdrew.

76
00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:48.000
The whole thing took a few minutes.

77
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:59.170
There was a civilian watching those few minutes from the slope of the Sapoune Ridge, from a point that commanded the entire battlefield: William Howard Russell, correspondent of the London newspaper The Times.

78
00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:07.140
Russell was one of the first war correspondents in the modern sense, and he wrote what he saw independently of the army's official dispatches.

79
00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:13.190
Describing the sight of that morning, he spoke of a thin red streak topped with a line of steel.

80
00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:21.160
Over the years the phrase shortened into the thin red line, and it is still used today for a minority holding its ground before a more numerous enemy.

81
00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.970
But the real cavalry achievement of the day had not yet come.

82
00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:33.100
The main body of the Russian cavalry, some 3 thousand horsemen, began to cross the Causeway Heights and descend into the South Valley.

83
00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:38.190
Just at that moment the British Heavy Brigade was on the march at the foot of the ridge.

84
00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:46.160
The Heavy Brigade was made up of cavalry on big horses, in breastplates or heavy uniforms, trained for the work of breaking and crushing.

85
00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:53.010
Its commander, Major General James Scarlett, was 55 and had never been in a battle in his life.

86
00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:58.420
When Scarlett saw the mass coming down at him he did what very few commanders would have done.

87
00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:02.390
He did not fall back and he did not try to take up a position.

88
00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:08.280
He gave his men the order to turn right, waited for them to dress their ranks, and charged uphill.

89
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.730
At that first moment he had only about 300 horsemen with him.

90
00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:18.940
In a cavalry charge the advantage lies almost always in speed and in the slope; Scarlett had neither.

91
00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:21.790
He drove into the Russian mass all the same.

92
00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:24.960
There followed a struggle of eight or ten minutes.

93
00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:32.450
It was a crowd on horseback, sabres locked together, horses screaming, nobody able to see where anyone else was.

94
00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:41.540
When the British squadrons coming up behind struck the mass in the flank, the Russian line came apart and the cavalry pulled back to the north of the Causeway Heights.

95
00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:45.190
British losses were 10 dead and 98 wounded.

96
00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:50.760
It was the best planned, coolest and least talked about piece of soldiering of that day.

97
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:57.770
While all this was happening, about a kilometre to the north-west of the Heavy Brigade, the Light Brigade stood without moving.

98
00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:08.460
As the Russian cavalry broke up and withdrew, these five fresh, rested regiments on their flank were ideally placed to pursue them; pursuit was precisely what light cavalry existed for.

99
00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:13.390
Their commander, Lord Cardigan, did not stir from his place.

100
00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:16.240
He said he had been ordered to hold his position.

101
00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:19.890
Discontented murmurs went round among his officers.

102
00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:25.940
And for one staff officer watching it all from headquarters, the sight was more than he could bear.

103
00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.470
It was coming up to 10 o'clock.

104
00:10:28.470 --> 00:10:34.840
The Russians were sitting on the Causeway Heights, hooking up the captured guns and preparing to draw them off.

105
00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:42.490
The commander in chief, Lord Raglan, stood on the crest of the Sapoune Ridge, some 200 metres above the floor of the valley.

106
00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:49.180
He was 66, he had lost his right arm at Waterloo, and in his youth he had served as Wellington's aide de camp;

107
00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:57.470
so much so that throughout this war he was said now and then to refer absent-mindedly to the enemy as the French, although the French were now his allies.

108
00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:02.080
From where Raglan stood the battlefield looked like a table.

109
00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:09.890
He could see the movement along the Causeway Heights, the Russians in the redoubts making ready to remove the guns, both valleys at once.

110
00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:17.060
Down below, on the flat, the commander of the Cavalry Division, Lord Lucan, could not see behind the ridge.

111
00:11:17.060 --> 00:11:26.310
A commander who cannot see what lies beyond a low hill and a commander looking down on the top of that hill from above do not understand the same thing when they read the same order.

112
00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:30.280
The whole tragedy of that morning is hidden in that one sentence.

113
00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:37.290
Raglan first sent an order: the cavalry was to advance when infantry support arrived and retake the redoubts.

114
00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:42.220
The infantry divisions were marching up from kilometres behind, and they were late.

115
00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:48.430
Raglan looked down, watched the Russians putting ropes on the guns, and saw the minutes running away.

116
00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:55.920
He had his chief of staff, Brigadier General Richard Airey, write out another order in pencil, resting it on his knee.

117
00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:58.130
It was four lines.

118
00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:09.140
The order said this: Lord Raglan wishes the cavalry to advance rapidly to the front, to follow the enemy and try to prevent the enemy carrying away the guns.

119
00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.630
Troop horse artillery may accompany.

120
00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:15.320
French cavalry is on your left.

121
00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:17.530
Immediate.

122
00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:21.020
There is not a single place name in that text.

123
00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:25.790
It does not say which enemy, which guns, which direction.

124
00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:34.720
Raglan had no need of any of it, because he was looking at the guns; he meant the British naval guns being dragged away from the redoubts on the Causeway Heights.

125
00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.730
The man the paper was going to could not see them.

126
00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:42.340
The task of carrying the order was given to Captain Louis Edward Nolan.

127
00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:49.510
Nolan was a cavalry officer of the 15th Hussars who had grown up in India and had written two books on cavalry tactics.

128
00:12:49.510 --> 00:13:01.000
He believed passionately that cavalry, properly used, could win a battle; and he took the way the British cavalry had been constantly held back in the Crimean campaign as something close to a personal insult.

129
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:04.810
He did not trouble to hide his opinions of Lucan and Cardigan.

130
00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:09.740
He came down the slope by a steep path, almost sliding his horse down it.

131
00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:13.710
Lucan took the order, read it, and looked at the ground in front of him.

132
00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:22.320
Before him lay the North Valley: an open corridor two kilometres long, a Russian battery at the end of it, Russian positions on either side.

133
00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:27.250
It was the worst piece of ground imaginable for cavalry.

134
00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:29.620
He asked which guns were meant.

135
00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:34.390
Nolan's answer, together with the sweep of his arm, went into history.

136
00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:39.480
He flung out his arm in a wide arc and said that there was the enemy, and there were the guns.

137
00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:43.290
To this day nobody knows exactly what his arm was pointing at.

138
00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.100
The direction he indicated was the far end of the North Valley.

139
00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:52.510
But perhaps he meant the redoubts along the ridge, and the arm he threw out in anger was misread.

140
00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:54.560
Lucan did not object.

141
00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:59.170
The paper said immediate, and the order came from the commander in chief.

142
00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:03.460
He rode over to the head of the Light Brigade and passed the order to Cardigan.

143
00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:08.390
That these two men had to speak to each other at all is a matter in itself.

144
00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:14.920
Lord Lucan and Lord Cardigan were brothers in law; Cardigan's sister was Lucan's wife.

145
00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:22.090
The marriage had gone badly, the family tie had turned into enmity, and for years the two men had barely spoken.

146
00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:30.540
Now one was a division commander and the other a brigade commander under him, and in the middle of a battle they addressed one another with formal coldness.

147
00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:38.350
Cardigan was 57, a man who had spent much of his fortune on his regiment's uniforms and who was known for his notion of discipline.

148
00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:41.680
He listened to the order and saluted in the saddle.

149
00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:52.210
Then he spoke, saying that with permission he wished to point something out to his brother in law: there was a battery at the foot of the valley, and on the hills on either side there were riflemen and guns.

150
00:14:52.210 --> 00:14:53.780
Lucan knew it.

151
00:14:53.780 --> 00:15:02.070
His reply passed on the weight of the sentence on the paper exactly as it stood: there was no choice, the order came from Lord Raglan.

152
00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:06.040
Cardigan muttered something to himself and turned his horse.

153
00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:11.290
He rode to the front of the brigade, drew his sword, and signalled to the trumpeter.

154
00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:13.340
It was about 11:10.

155
00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:25.150
The Light Brigade numbered 673 horsemen in five regiments: the 17th Lancers, the 13th Light Dragoons, the 11th Hussars, the 4th Light Dragoons and the 8th Hussars.

156
00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:30.400
The lancers and light dragoons were in the first line, with a second and a third line behind them.

157
00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:40.610
Cardigan rode ahead of his regiments, alone, two horse lengths in front; he wore the dark blue, gold laced uniform of the 11th Hussars, and the whole brigade had his back in view.

158
00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:43.140
The brigade set off at a trot.

159
00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:52.230
At the entrance to the valley everything still looked like a parade: the ranks even, the intervals measured, the hoofbeats falling into a single rhythm.

160
00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:57.160
About 200 metres on, a horseman broke out from the right of the line.

161
00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:00.010
It was Nolan.

162
00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:07.020
Having delivered the order, he had attached himself to the 17th Lancers to ride in the charge alongside his old comrades.

163
00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:14.670
Now he was cutting across in front of the brigade, in front of Cardigan, his sword raised, shouting something and pointing to the right.

164
00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:19.920
Cardigan took it for insolence, an attempt to force the pace, and was furious.

165
00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:26.130
Nobody could hear what Nolan was saying, because at that instant the first Russian shell from the right burst near him.

166
00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.820
A fragment tore his chest open.

167
00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:37.590
His sword stayed up in his hand, his body stayed bolt upright in the saddle, and the horse carried its dead rider back through the advancing line and away.

168
00:16:37.590 --> 00:16:42.520
The first casualty of the charge was the man who had brought the order that launched it.

169
00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:46.330
Whatever he had been trying to point out was buried with him.

170
00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:49.500
What followed lasted 20 minutes.

171
00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:56.510
On the right were the Causeway Heights, with the redoubts along them in Russian hands, and fire came in from that flank.

172
00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:04.000
On the left were the Fedyukhin Heights, where Russian guns and riflemen could take comfortable aim at a target inside the corridor.

173
00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:11.810
Ahead, at the foot of the valley, were 8 guns under Prince Obolensky, served by a detachment of 200 artillerymen.

174
00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:17.220
The fire coming from three directions was like three walls closing in on a moving box.

175
00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:21.190
The first round shot tore gaps in the lines.

176
00:17:21.190 --> 00:17:27.720
The most basic reflex of cavalry training took over: close the gap, close up, keep the dressing.

177
00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:34.890
Men edged inwards to fill the places of the comrades who had fallen, the line narrowed, straightened again, and kept going.

178
00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:38.380
The trot became a gallop.

179
00:17:38.380 --> 00:17:44.750
Cardigan rode on without altering his distance in front; as he would later say, he never looked behind him.

180
00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:52.240
When they reached the end of the valley the Russian battery fired its last salvo from very close range, from perhaps 50 metres.

181
00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:55.570
A curtain of smoke shut out everything at once.

182
00:17:55.570 --> 00:18:00.180
The riders of the first line went into the smoke and in among the guns.

183
00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:08.150
What happened there was no longer a charge but a struggle around the gun carriages, between the wheels, with sabres and artillery handspikes.

184
00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:13.400
Some of the Russian gunners left their pieces and ran; some fought where they stood.

185
00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:18.170
But behind the battery the main body of the Russian cavalry was waiting.

186
00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:27.580
The British horsemen who had reached the foot of the valley were now scattered, exhausted, a few hundred at most, and there was no longer any such thing as a brigade.

187
00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:38.270
What was left of the 17th Lancers and the 11th Hussars pressed on a little further, pushed back the mass of cavalry in front of them, and then, as the danger of being surrounded appeared,

188
00:18:38.270 --> 00:18:41.600
had to turn back in small groups.

189
00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:45.090
The way back was worse than the way in.

190
00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:54.020
They would come back down the same corridor, this time in disorder, their horses wounded, some of them on foot, and the guns on both sides were still there.

191
00:18:54.020 --> 00:18:59.430
What silenced the fire on the left was an intervention that was no part of any plan.

192
00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:07.720
The 4th Chasseurs d'Afrique, the French African light horse, under General d'Allonville, made a flank attack on the Fedyukhin Heights.

193
00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:15.050
This unit, seasoned in the fighting in Algeria, drove two half batteries and two infantry battalions off their positions on the slope.

194
00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:22.860
That quieted the left wall of the North Valley for a few critical minutes and opened a corridor through which the returning British cavalry could pass.

195
00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:27.470
It was this that saved a substantial part of the Light Brigade's survivors.

196
00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:32.880
The French marshal Pierre Bosquet watched all of it from a height alongside.

197
00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:39.570
The sentence he spoke became almost the second name of this charge: it was magnificent, but it was not war.

198
00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:44.500
The rarely quoted continuation was harder still: it was madness.

199
00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:49.110
Those who came back took the flanking fire from the Causeway Heights once more.

200
00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:57.240
Some of the wounded scattered across the valley floor were hunted by Cossack horsemen; some were carried out by comrades who took them up behind their saddles.

201
00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.890
Men who had lost their horses walked the two kilometres back.

202
00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:06.300
Cardigan had reached the battery, had been briefly engaged, and had ridden back alone.

203
00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:11.550
He did not gather his men and did not try to find out what had happened behind him.

204
00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:15.840
He left the battlefield and returned to his yacht in the harbour.

205
00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:24.770
When the trumpet sounded to assemble, out of a brigade that had set off with 673 horsemen, 195 were able to form up still mounted.

206
00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:32.100
Close to 110 men had been killed, about 130 were wounded, and some had been taken prisoner wounded.

207
00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:36.070
375 horses had been lost.

208
00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:41.960
Not a gun had been captured; the battery at the foot of the valley was back in Russian hands within minutes.

209
00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:45.610
The Causeway Heights were not retaken that day either.

210
00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:47.820
20 minutes.

211
00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:51.630
About as long as it takes a man to drink a cup of coffee.

212
00:20:51.630 --> 00:21:00.720
The second battle, the one that began after the fighting, lasted far longer than the first, and nobody died in it; only reputations died.

213
00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:10.610
In his official dispatch reporting the affair to London, Lord Raglan laid the responsibility squarely on Lucan: he wrote that what had been done did not correspond to what the order said,

214
00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:14.100
and that the division commander had grossly misinterpreted it.

215
00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:22.230
The dispatch was published in the official gazette in London, which meant that Lucan read the document accusing him at the same moment as the public did.

216
00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:30.040
He wrote a defence, exchanged further letters, and in March 1855 he was relieved of his command and recalled to England.

217
00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:40.570
On 2 March 1855 he rose in the House of Lords and defended himself; he read out the text of the order he had been given and explained that no other conclusion could be drawn from it.

218
00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:47.580
His career did not end that day; he went on being promoted over the years and rose in the end to the rank of field marshal.

219
00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:50.750
But his name never got free of those 20 minutes.

220
00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:55.040
Lord Cardigan, for his part, went home to England a hero.

221
00:21:55.040 --> 00:22:01.890
Crowds greeted him, he was made inspector general of cavalry, and he gave speeches describing the charge.

222
00:22:01.890 --> 00:22:10.820
Years later, in a book by an officer who had been in the battle, it was alleged that Cardigan had never in fact reached the Russian guns and had abandoned his men early.

223
00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:14.630
Cardigan took the matter to court in 1863.

224
00:22:14.630 --> 00:22:21.800
The case did not end in the clear vindication he had expected, and the argument over the accusation continued after his death.

225
00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:26.250
The easiest man to blame was Nolan, because Nolan was dead.

226
00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:30.540
A dead officer can answer none of the claims made against him.

227
00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:38.510
We will never know what his arm was pointing at, what the last sentence out of his mouth was, or whether, when he rode out in front in the middle of the charge,

228
00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:44.720
he was trying to hurry the brigade along or shouting that it was going the wrong way and trying to correct it.

229
00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:49.650
The story that blamed him settled comfortably into precisely that empty space.

230
00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:53.620
The same mechanism worked on another group of men that morning.

231
00:22:53.620 --> 00:23:03.350
In the British press and in memoirs, the Ottoman soldiers who had defended the redoubts on the Causeway Heights were quickly reduced to the cliché of the Turks who ran away;

232
00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:07.160
they became the cheap and convenient culprits of the story.

233
00:23:07.160 --> 00:23:18.650
And yet the documents of that same story also recorded how long the foremost redoubt had held out under Russian artillery and how much of its garrison it had lost; it was simply that nobody wanted to read that part.

234
00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:27.100
Historians working on the battle corrected the picture over the course of the twentieth century, but correcting a cliché takes much longer than creating one.

235
00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:31.070
The turning of the story into poetry happened very fast.

236
00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:36.160
Russell's detailed report from the Crimea appeared in The Times in the middle of November;

237
00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:44.610
to read of a disaster on the battlefield not from the government's mouth but from the pen of a journalist who had watched it was a new experience for the British public.

238
00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:49.700
One of those who read that report was Alfred Tennyson, Poet Laureate of the day.

239
00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:58.310
On 2 December 1854 he wrote a poem in a few minutes, and on 9 December it was published in the weekly paper The Examiner.

240
00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:01.480
Its title was The Charge of the Light Brigade.

241
00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:04.810
The poem did not debate the causes of the affair.

242
00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:16.620
It said that an order had been given, that someone had blundered, but that the soldier's business was not to question but to do and to die; and then it gave the corridor they rode down a name: the valley of Death.

243
00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:24.750
The following year, on the initiative of a clergyman, thousands of copies of the poem were printed and distributed to the soldiers in the Crimea.

244
00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:29.200
Within a few months a defeat had turned into a text people learned by heart.

245
00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:32.210
That transformation had its price.

246
00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:38.260
The poem made the event unforgettable, but it also covered over the real question the event raised.

247
00:24:38.260 --> 00:24:42.870
The real question was not courage; nobody doubted anyone's courage.

248
00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:51.640
The real question was this: how do you write, in four lines, an order describing ground you cannot see as though the man receiving it saw what you see?

249
00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:56.730
Should a commander tell his subordinate what to do, or what he is trying to achieve?

250
00:24:56.730 --> 00:25:07.740
This is why Balaclava is still taught in modern staff training; every one of those rules that look ordinary today, that intent should be stated plainly in orders, that the objective should be named,

251
00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:14.430
that the vantage point of the recipient should be taken into account, was written out of the bill for mornings like this one.

252
00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:17.440
One other strange legacy remained.

253
00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:21.090
Three words from that war outlived the men they came from.

254
00:25:21.090 --> 00:25:29.860
The knitted headgear sent from home in the freezing Crimean winter, leaving openings only for the eyes and mouth, was given the name of the harbour: balaclava.

255
00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:37.990
The sleeve cut from the shoulder down to the neckline so that Raglan's single arm would be comfortable entered the vocabulary of tailoring as the raglan sleeve.

256
00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:47.080
And the knitted jacket buttoned down the front, worn in Cardigan's regiment, took his name; today millions of people wear a cardigan knowing nothing whatever about that morning.

257
00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:57.930
The survivors met every 25 October for many years; in 1877 a society was founded for these gatherings, and at the annual dinners the tables emptied a little more each year.

258
00:25:57.930 --> 00:26:08.300
One of the last to go was Edwin Hughes, who had ridden in the charge as a private in the 13th Light Dragoons and who had been left in the valley with his leg pinned when his horse was killed under him.

259
00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:11.470
In the town where he lived they called him Balaclava Ned.

260
00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:17.040
He died on 18 May 1927, 72 years after the charge.

261
00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:27.570
With him went the last human being who had seen that valley with his own eyes; and there remained only the lines everyone had memorised, and a question nobody has ever quite been able to answer.

262
00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:31.060
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

263
00:26:31.060 --> 00:26:34.230
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
